//! Ground truth generated by `lean/`, and the exact arithmetic to check it with.
//!
//! The arrangement this crate serves: the Lean development is the specification, and for
//! the parts of the mathematics that are polynomial — the forward chain, the wrench
//! algebra, the dynamics — Lean can *evaluate* it over `ℚ` and emit the results. Those
//! vectors are exact, so the Rust implementation can be held to them with `==` rather
//! than against a tolerance band, and a disagreement is a real disagreement rather than
//! floating-point drift. `test_force_torque.py` already works this way; this is the Rust
//! counterpart, extended to the forward chain.
//!
//! The parts that are *not* polynomial — every angle, through `atan2`, `arccos` and
//! `cos` — cannot be treated this way, because their values are irrational outside a
//! handful of cases (Niven's theorem). Those are covered by restating the Lean theorems
//! as executable properties instead, which is what `docs/verification.md` sets out.
//!
//! Nothing here is linked into an application. It exists so the test suites can be
//! exact where exactness is available.

#![forbid(unsafe_code)]

pub mod numeral;
pub mod rational;

pub use numeral::{NumeralError, entries, matrix3, name, numeral, numeral_at, parse, vec3};
pub use rational::Ratio;
